            "source_quote": "καὶ ὁμῶς παλαίσας ἐπὶ τὴν ἰδίαν αὐτοῦ φύσιν βουληθεὶς ἐτράπη.",
            "english_quote": "After wrestling evenly with him, when the angel wished, he was transformed back into his own nature.",
            "issue": "“Evenly” selects an equal-match interpretation of ὁμῶς. The adverb can also mean “together” or “alike,” without asserting parity in the contest. The selected interpretation is defensible, but this materially different alternative is not disclosed.",
            "suggested_revision": "Add a source-quoted lexical note explaining the equal-match interpretation and preserving “after wrestling together with him” as an alternative. Alternatively, propose that wording in the main text and document the equality reading. Either revision requires fresh review; this is interpretive ambiguity, not source corruption.",
            "severity": "minor"
          },
          {
            "source_quote": "ἤδη εἰς πῦρ μεταβαλόμενος τοῦ Ἰακὼβ τὸ πλατὺ νεῦρον οὐκ ἔκαυσε μέν, ἔφλεξε δέ, καὶ χωλὸν ἐποίησεν.",
            "english_quote": "Then, changing into fire, he did not burn Jacob’s broad sinew, but scorched it and made him lame.",
            "issue": "“Then, changing” suppresses the already/by-that-time force of ἤδη with the aorist participle μεταβαλόμενος. It can suggest a subsequent transformation after the return to the angel’s own nature, whereas the Greek presents the change into fire as already accomplished at the relevant point.",
